## Color tokens

Colors are **space-separated RGB channels** (no commas) — applied as CSS variables:

```css
--orbit-canvas: 15 20 38;
background: rgb(var(--orbit-canvas));
```

| Token | CSS variable | Usage |
| ----- | ------------ | ----- |
| `canvas` | `--orbit-canvas` | App background, artwork scrims |
| `surface` | `--orbit-surface` | Inputs / nested surfaces, wallpaper gradient |
| `panel` | `--orbit-panel` | Cards and glass panels |
| `foreground` | `--orbit-foreground` | Primary text |
| `muted` | `--orbit-muted` | Secondary text |
| `accent` | `--orbit-accent` | CTA / highlights, wallpaper glow |
| `focus` | `--orbit-focus` | Controller focus ring |
| `border` | `--orbit-border` | Subtle borders |

Applied at runtime by `theme.store.ts` when user selects a theme. Shell, wallpaper, hero/detail scrims, focus rings and progress read these variables through Tailwind `orbit-*` utilities. Game and custom themes bring their own palette (`GAME_PALETTES` in `themes/catalog.ts`, `ThemeColorsEditor`); stored legacy palettes are not applied. The store also sets `html[data-theme]`, `html[data-theme-kind]`, and `--theme-wallpaper`.

Liquid Glass palette (`LIQUID_GLASS_COLORS`): canvas `6 6 8`, surface `14 14 16`, panel `22 22 25`, foreground `250 250 249`, muted `150 150 158`, accent `249 115 22` (orange), focus `250 204 21` (gold), border `58 58 62`.

### Onboarding visuals

Onboarding (see `docs/ONBOARDING.md`) is an overlay card floating over Orbit, which stays visible behind it, dimmed and blurred. The card (28px radius) carries its own scenery: warm corner glows, stars, and a planet whose orange rim glows along the horizon near the bottom with a sun bloom behind it. Header: Orbit logo left, minimize and ✕ right, five step dots centred below (done = white check, current = orange ring with glow, upcoming = hollow; lines turn orange as steps finish). Steps: a large centred title and subtitle, glass panels with hairline rows (`ONBOARDING_PANEL`, `PANEL_ROWS`), orange checkboxes and switches, badges (orange "Recommended"/"Default", quiet otherwise), a ← Back pill on the left and the orange primary with → on the right (centred on Welcome). Progress bars use `tone="fire"`; Scan shows a fan of covers beside its checklist.

### Empty states and loading

Every "nothing here" and "no results" uses `EmptyState` (`components/feedback/EmptyState.tsx`): a glass panel with a softly glowing, floating icon, a clear title, a hint at what to do, and optional actions that fix it (the first is the fire primary). `size="sm"` fits tables and small cards. Used by Library and Search (no matches: Clear search, plus Show all games when a view or platform narrows it; empty library: Add games, Library settings), Steam not-installed games, the Home shelf (per view, with Open library), Storage (items table, largest installs) and the Downloads sidebar.

Library loading placeholders (`LibrarySkeleton`) match the cards: the same grid (`LIBRARY_ENTRY_GRID` in `library/components/libraryGrid.ts`, shared with `LibraryEntryGrid`), the same glass card shell, square art, a title line and a status line with an icon dot, under a section-heading placeholder, all with the artwork loader's shimmer sweep.

No text loaders: nothing reads "Loading…", "Saving…" or "Scanning…". Content that is loading shows its shape as shimmer (`SkeletonBlock`, `SkeletonPanel` in `components/feedback/Skeleton.tsx`; `DetailsSkeleton` for game details, artwork tiles in image search and Theme settings, shelf tiles on Home), named for assistive tech by `role="status"` and `aria-label` only. A button whose action runs keeps its label and gains a spinner (`BusyLabel`, `components/feedback/BusyLabel.tsx`, `data-busy`), so it never changes width or wording; icon buttons spin their icon or swap it for `Spinner`. A bare `Spinner` (an orbit ring with a running arc, pulsing under reduced motion) stands in where there is no shape to show. Progress lines that report real numbers (download phases and percentages) stay.

### Startup splash

`index.html` carries the splash inline (`#orbit-splash`), so it paints before any script module: onboarding's scene in miniature, with no text. The Orbit logo floats in the middle over a breathing glow, above a planet's horizon sized like `planetGeometry` (rim at 84% of the height, leaving through the bottom corners). Meteors (18, already mid-flight on the first frame, with glowing heads and long tails) streak down and sparkles twinkle (both seeded, written by a small inline script), and nebula glows drift. The planet starts as onboarding's CSS photo planet (the Earth photo under a fire rim, atmosphere and sun bloom). As soon as the app's script runs, `app/splash/splashGlobe.tsx` mounts onboarding's 3D globe (`SpaceGlobe`, spinning faster here) into `#orbit-splash-globe` on a real GPU with motion on (`graphicsTier()`). The splash waits for its first drawn frame (at most 4.5 s), the globe fades in over the photo, and its WebGL scene is torn down once the splash has gone; elsewhere the photo planet stays.

`app/splash/splash.ts` dismisses the splash: `main.tsx` holds it until `App` mounts, and screens hold it while their first content loads (`useSplashHold`: Home's library, onboarding until it is ready; the splash globe until it has drawn). Once nothing holds it, after at least 3.2 s, the transition plays (about 1.9 s) (`orbit-splash--leaving` on the splash, `orbit-entering` on `html`):
- the logo (the HD artwork, `orbit-logo-hd.png`; its entrance only fades and rises, and it carries no CSS filter: a scale or a filter left it blurry in the app's WebKit) flies through the screen (scale 5, blur);
- the stars warp outward, and the planet and globe drop away;
- Home opens over the splash through an iris that starts at the logo (`clip-path` circle), out of a bright blur, with a fire ring riding the iris edge (`html::after`, same timing);
- the splash fades behind it.

A hold never released cannot keep the splash up past 10 s. Controller input is ignored until the splash has gone. Reduced motion uses short fades, with no iris or ring.

### Focus ring

`Focusable`'s ring (`ring-2` in the focus colour) and the switch's ring sit flush on the control. They used to have a 2 px offset filled with the canvas colour, which showed as a dark border between the control and its ring.

### Downloads sidebar

`SteamActivitySidebar` is a floating liquid glass sheet (inset 12 px from the window edge, 28 px radius, clear tint, bright top and left rims, light pooling inside the top edge) that springs in from the right over a blur overlay. The overlay fades in while its backdrop blur ramps to 14 px (animated through `--scrim-blur`, which both `backdrop-filter` spellings read, so older WebKit works); clicking it closes the sheet. The header, sections and network card cascade in, empty-state icons bob gently (`float-soft`), and the cards and rows share the glass control material. Sections, top to bottom: Network (live transfer speed), Downloads, Queue, and "Played, not installed" (up to five games you have played that have no available installation, most played first, then by recency; Steam ones install into the queue, others open their details). Each section has a one-line description under its title (`SectionHeading`). The header (title and close button) stays put; the sections scroll beneath it with a short fade at the top. The sheet's tint is dark (`rgb(10 8 14 / 0.66)`) and its glass dims the page behind it (`brightness(0.7)`), so it reads deeper than the blurred backdrop. Closing plays the exit through `DrawerProvider`'s `AnimatePresence` (drawers without an exit animation, such as Theme Studio, still close at once). Reduced motion swaps the spring, blur ramp and cascade for short fades and stops the bob.

### Home shelf filters

The Recent / Installed / Favorites filters and "View library" sit over the hero artwork, so their text is full white with a soft shadow (`0 1px 2px` at 55% black), and the filter group has a light dark tint, keeping them legible over bright images. The selected filter adds a white glass fill.

### Buttons and the fire gradient

Primary actions use the brand fire gradient in every theme: `--orbit-fire-from` `255 84 62` (red-orange), `--orbit-fire-via` `255 120 48` (orange), `--orbit-fire-to` `255 164 72` (amber), with a white label (`--orbit-fire-ink`) and a faint text shadow. These live on `:root` in `styles/index.css` and `theme.store.ts` never writes them, so game themes cannot recolour a primary button. `.orbit-btn-fire` is liquid glass tinted with the gradient: slightly translucent over a light backdrop blur and saturation lift, a gloss over the upper half, a bright top rim, a hairline inner edge and a darker lower edge. Hover brightens it, press scales it down, and disabled (busy states such as "Installing…") is neutral glass; reduced motion drops the press. There is no moving sheen. `.orbit-progress-fire` is the matching progress fill (`ProgressBar tone="fire"`).

Build buttons with `buttonClass({ variant, size, shape })` from `components/ui/button.tsx` (pass it to `Focusable`'s `className`) or the plain `Button`: `primary` (fire), `secondary` (glass), `quiet`, `danger`; sizes `sm`/`md`/`lg`; `pill` or `card` corners. Selected tabs, chips, switches and the home white Play pill are not primary buttons and keep their own styles.

### Theme JSON shape

```typescript
interface ThemeDefinition {
  id: string
  name: string
  description?: string
  author?: string
  fonts?: { display?: string; body?: string }
  colors: ThemeColors
  radius?: string
  cardRadius?: string
  kind?: 'color' | 'game' | 'custom'
  blur?: string
  motion?: { duration?: string; easing?: string }
  branding?: {
    logoText?: string       // legacy, not rendered
    logoStyle?: 'clean' | 'serif' | 'gothic' | 'glitch' | 'heavy' | 'neon' | 'rounded'
    logoImage?: string      // inline raster data URL
    wallpaper?: string      // inline raster data URL
    tagline?: string
    artworkQuery?: string
  }
}
```

See [docs/THEMES.md](THEMES.md) for authoring walkthrough.

