## Rules

1. **Tokens first** — use CSS variables (`rgb(var(--orbit-accent))`) not hardcoded hex in components.
2. **Theme-aware** — new UI must read palette variables so every color and game theme recolors it.
3. **Focusable everything** — interactive elements must register with `Focusable` or equivalent.
4. **Couch contrast** — keep text readable at a distance; test with `--orbit-muted` for secondary copy.
5. **Glass panels** — use `LiquidGlass` or `.orbit-panel`; prefer `backdrop-filter` over opaque overlays. Layout, type, and spacing live as Tailwind classes on the component; tokens stay CSS variables in `styles/index.css`.
6. **Document theme fields** — adding JSON theme properties requires updating [docs/THEMES.md](THEMES.md) and `ThemeDefinition` in `src/themes/types.ts` and `src-tauri/src/models/theme.rs` (plus sanitization in `services/theme_files.rs`).

## Settings viewport layout
Top-level navigation tabs have no Back button; detail flows retain Back. Settings
fills the space between shell header and footer with aligned panels. Only the middle
panel scrolls. Below 1180px the insights panel hides; below 520px tabs form a row
above the content. Windowed headers reserve titlebar space vertically, preserving logo alignment.

Reduce Motion replaces artwork immediately after it decodes. Reduce Transparency uses opaque glass panels.


### Portable glass materials

Orbit runs only in macOS's WebKit view, where SVG displacement filters in `backdrop-filter` (the usual web refraction) are unsupported, so refraction is built from light rather than distortion. The shared material (`styles/index.css`): a 5 px panel / 7 px toolbar / 4 px control blur (`glass_material.rs`), `saturate(1.7–2)` with a small brightness and contrast lift, a 42% surface tint, a diagonal specular gradient, a bright 1 px top rim with softer side and bottom rims, light pooling inside the top edge and a darker lower inner edge. The grain tile is no longer drawn. Reduce Transparency and high contrast still switch to opaque surfaces.

The shared `LiquidGlass` surface uses 12px toolbar blur and 8px panel blur,
subtle edge highlights, and the existing artwork-aware tint. Rust renders one
64×64 PNG grain tile on a blocking worker and caches it for the process. A single
bootstrap query shares the texture with the shell and portaled drawers.
All translucent UI surfaces share the same material. Panels and connected-library cards use the 8px panel treatment. Buttons, badges, and translucent text fields use a compact 6px control treatment. Opaque artwork faces and scene-dimming scrims keep their own paint.
There are no per-panel distortion maps, SVG refraction passes, or resize observers.
Reduced transparency, increased contrast, and forced colors switch to opaque
surfaces dynamically. Unsupported blur compositors use a near-opaque fill.

These are shared webview surfaces. Rust prepares their material texture. OS-native
window materials have platform-specific behavior and do not supply a common
per-panel API for the three supported desktop webviews.

The `orbit-glass-control` material class preserves semantic HTML controls and tinted selection states. The `orbit-panel` utility uses the same material as `LiquidGlass`. Dialogs, session panels, and quick menus share the texture and accessibility fallbacks.


### Image readiness and sizing

`ArtworkImage` reserves its container before loading, decodes asynchronously,
and crossfades only when ready. Failed images stop shimmering and expose an
accessible fallback. Theme cards align their 2:1 image regions independently of
title wrapping or selection state. Logos use containment; heroes use cover.
Native preview generation trims transparent logo padding before GPU scaling.

Bundled display assets and previews use WebP. Logos are alpha-trimmed and capped at 1024×512; wallpaper resolution is preserved up to 3840×2160. Custom image preparation runs on a Rust worker before download caching or saving.

## Windows compatibility

The existing Settings rail includes Runtimes. Add game → Single file keeps its
current steps and shows Wine selection for `.exe` files. Edit properties reuses
that editor. Advanced arguments/diagnostics stay collapsed by default. Game details
keeps the existing Play/Stop controls, shows preparation text and cancellation,
and offers Compatibility diagnostics. See [RUNTIMES.md](RUNTIMES.md).

### Settings usability and download feedback

Settings uses a dedicated, responsive navigation rail and readable content surface. The rail includes Steam, with the authorization card and offline-mode and file-verification switches. The rail retains restrained LiquidGlass; the main form uses an opaque theme surface. The active section is a filled glass pill (inset highlight, not a hollow accent outline) that slides with Aceternity's shared-layout indicator and respects reduced motion. shadcn/Radix switches provide labeled keyboard-operable graphics toggles. Game graphics has sectioned controls and a sticky Save/Discard bar; unsaved drafts survive section navigation for the app session. Compatibility lists icon-led component/version rows with installed status and size. Native download events drive determinate transfer progress; preparation and installation are indeterminate. Failures expose retry without reporting a completed install. Unmanaged catalog versions are explicitly browse-only.

**Storage** (`modules/settings/components/storage/`) shows what Orbit keeps on the Mac: an overview bar of Games (orange), Tools (purple), Runtimes (blue) and Other (grey) against the rest of the disk, the three largest games, a distribution card, and a table of every item with type chips, search, sort, Show in Finder and remove. Games uninstall through the shared `UninstallDialog`; an unused component version can be removed; Steam for Windows, caches and library data are shown but cannot be removed there. Games are measured from their real folders (`services/install_roots.rs`): the folder is found from the launch path (`steamapps/common/<game>`, an Orbit container `install/`, `steam-windows/games/<appId>`, a `.app` bundle, else the launch file's own folder unless that is a broad place like Downloads or home), and a symlinked folder is followed to where the files live. Two library rows pointing at the same files count once. Those folders are skipped when the rest of Orbit's data is walked, so a game inside the Steam client's prefix counts as that game, not as Steam for Windows. Each row shows its source (`Orbit`, `Steam library`, `This Mac`); only folders on Orbit's drive fill the disk bar. Games installed outside Orbit are shown but not removable there. A scan is cached until something is installed or removed, and **Rescan** walks the folders again. Usage over time is not tracked yet.

Library tiles read `Installed · 60 GB` when the size is known. Details measure **Disk usage** from the game's real folder when opened (`get_install_sizes`, cached; "Measuring…" until it returns) with the download size beneath it, list the **Game folder**, and offer **Uninstall** for games Orbit downloaded. A measurement is saved to the game's row, so library tiles show it after a restart.

## Interface standards rollout

Library view controls are Installed / All games, with Favorites only when at least
one game is marked favorite. Initial loads use
shared skeletons; background refresh keeps the current cards visible with a compact
status message. Horizontal home shelves hide scrollbar chrome locally while retaining
scrolling.

Game details prioritize game artwork over theme wallpaper. Overview and Settings
remain within the same details page; Settings owns General, Artwork, and Launch &
Compatibility. Save keeps the editor open. The Downloads sidebar contains
account/network/transfer information and links to Library for owned-game browsing.

Shared Skeleton and Radix-based Select components use Orbit tokens. The remaining
component migration, controller integration for select menus, navigation guards,
and native WebKit visual review are still outstanding.

Details and the Library view selector share `TabList`, a Liquid Glass pill matching the header’s
rounded active segment and neutral highlight. Library search and sort/platform sit in
their own glass clusters beside that pill (48px couch height), not nested inside one bar.
The Library/Search toolbar sticks under the console header once it reaches that edge.
Covers and list rows dissolve through a viewport mask under the toolbar and above
the footer; the wallpaper is not dimmed by a dividing band. The mask updates on
the grid compositor (rAF-batched, no React re-renders while scrolling).
Controller focus rings remain separate from the selected state. Editor Save/Discard actions
flow inline with the form; they have no opaque or sticky footer surface.

Settings → Library is three cards: **Steam library folders** (each saved folder as a row
with its library name, full path and a remove button, an empty state, **Scan installed
games** in the card header, and the path field with Browse / Add), **Switch content**,
and **Clear library** in a rose danger card. Shared styles live in
`modules/settings/components/libraryStyles.ts`. It accepts additional Steam library roots or a
folder picker and supports removal. Saved folders update installed-game detection;
new download destinations are unchanged. Settings form controls participate in
spatial focus: D-pad moves controls, A activates switches/buttons or focuses text,
Left returns to the active section and Right enters its first enabled control.

Adding an additional Steam folder now starts a Windows import scan and reports the
result. Existing configured folders can be rescanned with **Scan installed games**.
Owned-library badges refresh after the scan. A failed scan keeps the saved folder
and offers a retry; new downloads retain their existing destination.

Homepage filters are Recent / Installed, with Favorites only when at least one
game is marked favorite. Each uses a bounded backend
query so installed and favorite games are not limited to the first all-games page.
The carousel spans the full viewport with no horizontal padding; the hero and
section header retain their gutters. It retains native horizontal scrolling and
Focusable buttons. A vertical mouse wheel over the shelf scrolls it sideways
(`useShelfWheel`) on a slightly underdamped spring (`lib/shelfSpring.ts`): it
overshoots by about a tenth, settles in about half a second, and lands on the
next card in the wheel's direction 120 ms after the wheel stops, so a small nudge
moves one card and a big flick several (instant when reduced motion is
requested; a controller focus scroll or native swipe takes over). Horizontal wheels and trackpad swipes stay native, and a finite
shelf at its edge lets the wheel scroll the page. Screen edges fade artwork through a CSS mask only while the
strip overflows (left, right, or both); a short shelf stays fully visible.
Shelves with seven or fewer tiles stay inset with the hero gutter, keep labels
fully visible, and reset horizontal scroll when switching from a looping shelf.
Looping shelves still dissolve into the footer through a viewport mask.
There is no particle or WebGL disintegration. Labels and focus indicators
remain intact. The hero title uses measured single-line fitting; other hero
content and actions retain their existing layout.

The home shelf loops in either direction using three bounded cycles when more
than seven games are shown, repeated to fill wide windows. Scroll rebasing
shifts any in-flight focus scroll so the shelf keeps moving forward instead of
jumping back to the start of a cycle. Keyboard/controller focus eases a card
inside the screen edges over 640ms (instant when reduced motion is requested).
Decoded thumbnail requests are shared across loop copies and held in a 64-entry LRU
